d $t \\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t = 2s$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180101 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "babebacc-c620-5336-84f2-4d7aa7dcc6d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180105 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180105 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163663" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180105 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $3kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $(t^{2} + t + 1)m$, where $t \\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t = 0s$ and $t = 2s$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180105 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "93920b26-fb04-52d5-8d58-1e17f6af55c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180106 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180106 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163665" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180106 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $2kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $6xN$ acting in the direction of increasing $x$. Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v = 4m/s$ at $x = 1$, calculate the velocity of the particle at $x = 2$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180106 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f5f6801-1e10-5d42-880a-19ee72d417ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180107 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180107 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163667" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180107 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $1kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(3x^{2} + 1)N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x$. Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at the origin, and the particle has velocity $v = 4m/s$ at $x = 1$, calculate the particle's initial velocity." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180107 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c1be8fb-89db-5bdf-8f42-bf7da0962b48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180573 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180573 #edb/ref 1061 45122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180573 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fa2ce148-bfaa-55f3-a775-56631c5bd980" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180573 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180574 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180574 #edb/ref 1061 45123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180574 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cb90c47f-c22d-5b7f-a72a-cfc50bb4fdcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180574 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180575 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180575 #edb/ref 1061 45124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180575 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "54dddd69-accb-5eb8-beac-e9f9dbc87bf7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180575 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180576 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180576 #edb/ref 1061 45125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180576 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "496c1449-0914-5643-b8b4-95ec09fb5867" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180576 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180577 #edb/ref 1061 45126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180577 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b02a7662-acf4-57da-98d7-bae7093076f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180577 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180578 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180578 #edb/ref 1061 45179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180578 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3bec1351-7ee4-5f29-8760-5dd679edda2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180578 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180579 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180579 #edb/ref 1061 45180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180579 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4d3d3f00-8ddb-5882-984a-2f48d2b19da6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180579 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180764 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we'll discuss the concepts of **work** and **kinetic energy** and describe how these concepts are related. \n\nThe **work done by the force** $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from the position $x=x_1$ to the position $x=x_2$ along the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\nW = \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} F(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons ($\\textrm{N}$) and meters ($\\textrm{m}$), respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nFor example, suppose a particle $P$ is moving along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $4x^3\\,\\textrm{N},$ acting in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below. Distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters.\n\n![](images/3f/3f0040d7a71563304cb954908988c82df1774a1197a5ce16c74a3b8ac65e04a0.png)\n\nLet's calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\nSubstituting the given values into our formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =16-1 \\\\  & =15\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the work done by $F$ in moving $P$ from $x_1=1\\,\\textrm m$ to $x_2=2\\,\\textrm m$ equals $15\\,\\textrm J.$\n\nWe can use the concept of work in conjunction with Newton's second law. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180764 #edb/ref 1065 7799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180764 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "33cf6fa3-5f8d-579b-990b-ddeb9d1e3b8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180764 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180765 #edb/ref 1064 "The **kinetic energy** of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE} = \\dfrac12mv^2.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms ($\\rm{kg}$) and meters per second ($\\rm{m/s}$), respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$). \n\nFor example, suppose a particle $P$ of mass $5\\,\\rm{kg}$ is moving along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of $3t^2\\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ where $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Let's calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=2\\,\\rm{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & ={t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0 = 0^3 + C\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nC = 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = t^3.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=2\\,\\rm{s}$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(2) = 2^3 = 8\\,\\rm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot {8}^{2} \\\\  & =160\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Kinetic energy is a *scalar* quantity.\n\n- You probably noticed that kinetic energy and work have the same units. This is not a coincidence! We'll learn more about that shortly.\n\n- In practical applications, it's often more important to calculate a *change* in the kinetic energy of a particle between two points in time. \n\nThe **change in kinetic energy** of a particle with mass $m,$ **initial velocity** $v_0,$ and **final velocity** $v_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta \\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12mv_1^2 - \\dfrac12mv_0^2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180765 #edb/ref 1065 7811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180765 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "42f83ae3-3805-546b-a5bf-264b8b56d6ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180765 #edb/ref 1067 "Kinetic Energy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180766 #edb/ref 1064 "The **work-energy principle** states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW = \\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nFor example, let a particle [math]P[/math] of mass [math]5\\,\\rm{kg}[/math] is moving along the [math]x[/math]-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is [math]4t\\,\\rm{m/s},[/math] where [math]t \\geq 0[/math] is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times [math]t=1\\,\\rm{s}[/math] and [math]t=2\\,\\rm{s}.[/math]\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by [math]v(t)=4t,[/math] where [math]t[/math] is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at [math]t=1[/math], is\n\\[\nv_0 = v(1) = 4(1) = 4\\,\\rm{m/s},\n\\]\nand final velocity, at [math]t=2[/math], is\n\\[\nv_1 = v(2) = 4(2) = 8\\,\\rm{m/s}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n\\begin{align*}\nW\n&= \\Delta\\, \\text{KE} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac12 \\cdot 5 \\cdot (8)^2 - \\dfrac12 \\cdot 5 \\cdot (4)^2 \\\\[5pt]\n&= 160 - 40 \\\\[5pt]\n&= 120\\,\\rm{J}.\n\\end{align*}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180766 #edb/ref 1065 7812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180766 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e00f462b-c087-5c42-afc7-bfeffad5906b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180766 #edb/ref 1067 "The Work-Energy Principle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389435 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389435 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163671" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389435 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389435 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $(t+1)\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=2\\,s$ and $t=4\\,s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389435 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8305d2ef-8fb9-5f80-b2fe-3b5b2380f4bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389435 #edb/ref 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=t+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(2)=2+1=3\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=4$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(4)=4+1=5\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (5)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (3)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =8\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389436 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389436 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389436 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389436 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389436 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389436 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389436 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389436 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186389441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389436 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c13537c9-cba9-528c-81df-ec823b958306" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389437 #edb/ref 1080 "25\\,J" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389437 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389437 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "70f35aeb-2053-5b37-abce-48d3972b397c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389438 #edb/ref 1080 "16\\,J" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389438 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389438 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "24f06dbe-e866-519e-8599-01c0ead2742e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389439 #edb/ref 1080 "9\\,J" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389439 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389439 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7a155385-200c-550f-96bf-08d5a75d20fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389440 #edb/ref 1080 "17\\,J" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389440 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389440 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "318dfa74-61f2-571e-8498-0ef0bc26ce59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389441 #edb/ref 1080 "8\\,J" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389441 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389441 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "156b8685-b780-52be-a010-0b4b0806771e" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
